[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Generics in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics allow you to define type-safe data structures and algorithms without committing to a specific data type. They provide a way to reuse code with different types without the overhead of boxing and unboxing or the risk of runtime type errors. Generics increase code reusability, type safety, and performance."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Use Generics?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Type Safety: Generics enforce type safety at compile time, preventing runtime type errors."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Code Reusability: Write code once and use it with different data types."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Performance: Avoid boxing and unboxing operations, which can improve performance."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Generic Classes"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"A generic class is a class that can operate on different types. You define a generic class by specifying type parameters within angle brackets '<>' after the class name."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"public class GenericClass<T> {
 private T _value;

 public GenericClass(T value) {
 _value = value;
 }

 public T GetValue() {
 return _value;
 }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'T' is the type parameter. You can replace 'T' with any valid data type when you create an instance of the class."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"GenericClass<int> intObject = new GenericClass<int>(10);
int intValue = intObject.GetValue(); // intValue is 10

GenericClass<string> stringObject = new GenericClass<string>("Hello");
string stringValue = stringObject.GetValue(); // stringValue is "Hello""},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Generic Methods"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"A generic method is a method that can operate on different types. You define a generic method by specifying type parameters within angle brackets '<>' before the return type."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"public static T GenericMethod<T>(T input) {
 return input;
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can call a generic method by specifying the type parameter explicitly or by letting the compiler infer the type."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"int intResult = GenericMethod<int>(5); // Explicitly specifying the type
string stringResult = GenericMethod("World"); // Type inference"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Generic Interfaces"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generic interfaces allow you to define contracts that can work with different types. This is useful for creating reusable components."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"public interface IGenericInterface<T> {
 T DoSomething(T input);
}"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"public class MyClass : IGenericInterface<int> {
 public int DoSomething(int input) {
 return input * 2;
 }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Constraints"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Constraints limit the types that can be used as type arguments. You can use the 'where' keyword to specify constraints."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"public class GenericClass<T> where T : class {
 // T must be a reference type
}"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Common constraints include 'class' (reference type), 'struct' (value type), 'new()' (must have a parameterless constructor), and base class or interface constraints."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"public class GenericClass<T> where T : IComparable<T>, new() {
 // T must implement IComparable<T> and have a parameterless constructor
}"},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics are a powerful feature in C# that enhance type safety, code reusability, and performance. By using generic classes, methods, and interfaces, you can write more flexible and maintainable code."}]
